Done. The 12-layer checkpoint is at `out/T1/model.safetensors` with 86 tensors, produced by one execution of `out/T1/solution.py`, which passed on the first run.

**Approach.** A plain script over the safetensors API. It reads both input shards via the index's weight map, drops blocks 2, 6, 10 and 14, and builds a fresh dict keyed by the new names from an old-to-new index map computed once from the ordered survivor list. Building a new dict instead of renaming in place removes the collision hazard, and a destination-collision check is still enforced.

**Checks enforced before anything is written, and again on the re-read file:**
- no tensor of blocks 12 to 15 remains, and surviving indices are exactly 0..11;
- exactly 12 `q_proj` tensors, and 7 tensors per block;
- exactly 86 tensors, with the two non-block tensors present;
- every output tensor is bit-identical in shape, dtype and values to its source tensor.

The output is staged to a temp file, re-read, re-verified, then renamed into place, so any failing check exits non-zero and leaves no output. An independent spot-check confirmed new block 2 holds old block 3 and new block 11 holds old block 15.

The self-report is at `out/T1/REPORT.md`. It lists safetensors 0.5.3 and torch 2.14.0 as the tools used, and explains why mergekit, torch-state-bridge and transformers were not needed.
